    "lawCode": "Amd §34, Chap 91 of 2002; amd §17, Chap 345 of 2009; amd §§2851, 2852, 2590-q, 2590-r, 2590-g & 2854, Ed L",
    "lawSection": "Education Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S5973\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend chapter 91 of the laws of 2002\namending the education law and other laws relating to the\nreorganization of the New York city school construction authority,\nboard of education and community boards, in relation to the\neffectiveness of certain provisions of such chapter; to amend chapter\n345 of the laws of 2009 amending the education law relating to the New\nYork city board of education, chancellor, community councils and\ncommunity superintendents, in relation to the effectiveness of certain\nprovisions of such chapter; and to amend the education law, in\nrelation to the New York city community school district system\n\nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:  This bill will provide a one year\nextension of mayoral control in New York City, while ensuring that the\nschool system is operating in an open and transparent manner to ensure\naccountability, and expands access to charter schools.\n\nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 and 2: Extends mayoral control in New York City and other\ncurrent sections of Article 52-A of the Education Law for one year to\nJune 30, 2016.\n\nSection 3: Allows charter schools that are currently authorized by the\nNew York City Department of Education to a different charter\nauthorizing entity.\n\nSection 4: Removes regional caps placed on charters granted, and\nreturns charters that were previously granted but not currently being\nused to the pool of available charters.\n\nSection 5: Requires the New York City Department of Education to\nreport on the distribution of specific types of funding by individual\nschool and per pupil.\n\nSection 6: Makes budget and expenditure reports of New York City\nDepartment of Education schools more readily available by placing them\non the department's website.\n\nSection 7: Requires the Department of Education to maintain certain\ninformation to be made available to members and officers of the\nExecutive and Legislature.\n\nSection 8: Provides for an enrollment preference for children of\ncharter school employees accounting for up twenty percent of newly\nadmitted students.\n\nSection 9: Allows charter schools to employ individuals, up to the\ngreater of thirty percent of total teaching staff or five, that meet\ncertain exceptional statutory criteria, but lack teaching\ncertification.\n\nSection 10: Requires the mayor of New York City to submit an education\nbudget plan to the Director of the budget, the temporary president of\nthe Senate, and the speaker of the Assembly for approval.\n\n\nJUSTIFICATION:  The current system of mayoral control in New York City\nis set to expire June 30, 2015. A one year extension of such control\nwill ensure progress is made, especially for New York City's most\nneediest schools, and allow the Legislature to adequately re-examine\nthe issue of extending mayoral control after such progress has been\ndemonstrated.\n\nIn the 2015 enacted budget alone New York City received a total of\n$9.2 billion in state aid for the 2015-16 school year. It is critical\nthat city and state stakeholders have access to the distribution of\nstate aid by the Department of Education to the 32 community school\ndistricts, approximately 1,650 schools and one million students.\nState aid to New York City schools has increased by an average of over\neight percent annually since 2012 while the New York City local\ncontribution to its schools has increased an average of five percent\nsince 2012. While there have been gains made under mayoral control,\nthere is a need for greater transparency in relation to the\ndistribution of state, local and federal funding. Across the State,\nthe distribution of funding by individual schools is maintained in an\nopen and accessible manner. This transparency provides a level of\naccountability that ensures New York State's struggling schools are\nadequately funded. The 2015 enacted budget specifically identified and\naddressed 178 struggling schools of which there are 91 in New York\nCity. A more targeted understanding of where state, local, and federal\nresources are allocated is necessary in addressing the issues these\nindividual schools face. Extending transparency to New York City\nschools will provide the same kind of accountability that exists in\nthe rest of the state.\n\nEliminating the arbitrary regional caps placed on the locating of\ncharter schools will further school choice for students within New\nYork City, and across the State. These schools help to provide quality\naccess to education, not only in New York City, but in areas across\nthe State. Increasing access to charter schools, that are held\naccountable by the charter renewal process, will allow students the\nopportunity to attend some of the most successful and highest\nachieving schools in the State.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:  New Bill.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:  None.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:  This act shall take effect immediately, provided that\nsections four, five, and six shall be deemed to have been in full\nforce and effect since January 1, 2012.\n\n",
